colz0101	Bret: We stole a lot of the really good things out of SCUMM and kept them. We kept the idea of the chore tool, which I just call CHORTLE, but they had CYST and BILE and all these bodily humor-flavored tools from SCUMM. CYST, BILE, FLEM, MMUCUS, and we kept-- But the, there are analogues, like the chore tool is the really big one, but it's the analogue to what they did in CYST which was the costume editor for all the other adventure games. And we kept those paradigms, you know they manipulate 3-D elements and 2-D elements and movies and things together on one timeline, but that really is harkening back to exactly what they were doing in 2-D games.
colz0102	Kevin: Lucas[Arts] is remembered for the great art and the funny writing and all of that, but it's all built on--
colz0103	Bret: Yeah, it's good tech.
colz0104	Kevin: Just amazing tech.
codp0101	Tim: One weird trivia fact about the world that I learned, I was um, I saw a couple of Manny Calavera tattoos when I was at PAX. And someone asked me, "Why are there no Glottis tattoos?" I started Googling and I couldn't find a single Glottis tattoo on the Internet. I found many Manny tattoos. And so I don't know why Glottis-- I'll just say that on the Internet tomorrow.
codp0102	Peter M: That there need to be more Glottis tattoos, I agree with that.
codr0101	Peter M: I was gonna say about the music, where he reveals the whole big conspiracy theory, that was a jam between the cello player and the, um, the contrabass clarinet player.
codr0102	Tim: Oh yeah.
codr0103	Peter M: Basically, the idea was that since it's a whole complicated conspiracy, that they would just jam and jam forever on the same theme, kind of weaving it out.
codr0104	Tim: Mm hm.
codr0201	Tim: You can't hear what he's listening-- What is Domino listening to on his headphones, in your mind?
codr0202	Peter M: Oh, he's li-- It's not just what he's--
codr0203	Tim: You can actually hear like a little--
codr0204	Peter M: He's listening to his own theme, and it's done as a headphone-- We mixed it so it would sound like headphones. It's very quiet, but he's listening to his own music.
coac0101	Dave: One thing that was a little weird animating all this underwater stuff: when you animated a little section like Manny jumping off the conveyor belt, or climbing up a chain, or getting dragged along by a boat or whatever, you would always try to add that element of the physics of being underwater. And then seeing the harsh contrast of when you're back in interactive mode and we're still using the same animation of him walking above water, it was hard for an animator to get over that. These little things you can't let go of.
coac0102	Mark: You would have had to do a whole new set of anims, like...
coac0103	Dave: And that, well, the other thing is we may not have, that may not have been the... The thing that would've stopped something like that. It could've even been, like... There would be too much code required to switch a system or something. Like, who knows what the reason was.
coei0101	Clint: Thinking back to the way that we all worked together at LucasArts. You know, the way the different departments collaborated. You know, it was-- Everybody just assumed that we were all rooting for each other and on the same team, and there was almost no sense of kind of trial period or-- Nowadays, and for a long time now in the video game industry, a lot of times when you are in a service capacity, such as sound or audio/music, uh, there's really a vetting period where what you're presenting is being vetted by the directors and producers and you need to hear back. What did they think? Are they going to want to continue to work with us, and that kind of thing. And that's fine. It's the way the film industry is, that's the way things are. But back then, especially at LucasArts, it was not at all like that. Everyone just wanted to be working together. It was all one team. Everyone was facing in the same collaborative direction and achieving fun results. I think that creates an atmosphere in which people feel comfortable creating and taking risks. So it paid off, I think.
cogh0101	Dave: I remember being really-- See how their hands kind of clip through their sleeves? I remember being really annoyed by that, as an animator. Having to settle to do it 'cause you just, you couldn't get the poses and the lines you needed... uh... without making things kind of collide. It's something we just had to kind of live with.
cogh0102	Peter T: And something we still live with today.
cogh0201	Mark: So that chain that's holding the ship. At one point we had to add joints to that because I think at one point it's animated. And it kept crashing and we couldn't figure out why, and we eventually figured out that you couldn't have more than ninety-nine joints in a rig in Softimage at that point. It had like a hundred joints or something.
cogh0202	Paul: No, but again it's this funny thing when people maybe see this stuff and they think of movies now that maybe look better, but we were having to deal with a lot of limitations.
cogh0203	Dave: I'm actually really, really excited to see the Remastered version because, you know, you look at elements of this game that was made over fifteen years ago and visually it still kind of holds up to games that you can see today. So I'm curious about what it's going to look like after Double Fine gets finished with it. I think it'd be pretty special.
covi0101	Darragh: It's funny, the casting of Manny was... It was tough because... Obviously you have-- I wanted to hire somebody who was legitimately Hispanic, and not somebody putting on a fake accent.
covi0102	Julian: Oh, for sure.
covi0103	Darragh: And normally in sort of the casting process you'd sit down with the project leader. You'd probably have five to ten candidates that you'd be looking at, and with Manny we had two. And Tony was more Humphrey Bogart and the actor was more comedic, and I was terrified that Tim was going to go the comedic route because I thought Tony was the guy. I remember printing out r╙sum╙s for the two guys and Tony had a lot more experience. Then there was a really cool picture of Tony taken from Havana where he's sitting up at the bar, and he just looked cool. I made sure that went in, too, just to help kind of push it in the direction I wanted it to go.
covi0104	Julian: And it's just, it's the perfect voice.
covi0105	Darragh: Yeah, yeah, he's great.
covo0101	Tim: There was a-- The elevator in my dorm that you could... push open while it was moving. You push the gate open, it freezes it in between floors, and hang out in the elevator for no reason. But there was a little, like a little, like, strip of metal up in the corner that that door would plug into when it was closed to know that it was closed. And we tried to get the elevator moving with the door open by sticking-- We're sticking our fingers in this thing, trying to trigger the little switch. I totally got electrocuted because it was like a live, actual electrical current in there.
covo0102	Peter M: Yeah, ouch!
covo0103	Tim: So that's where that comes from. That little door closing thing.
covo0104	Peter M: From ill-advised college activities?
covo0105	Tim: Ill-advised, drunken college dorm elevator near-death pranks.
cobl0101	Dave: The animation team was very green and we were lucky to work under people like Pete [Tsaykel] and some other senior folks on the team, like Eric Ingerson and Mark Hamer. We came out of school learning traditionally 2-D animation and probably had about a month or two of work on "Monkey Island 3," I think, which was still semi-traditional. And then this was our first kind of interaction with the 3-D and computers and we struggled. We watched Eric do the most complex things you can imagine.
cobl0102	Peter T: It was all very new. I think we had a couple of consulting sessions. This was back when we were still kind of connected to ILM, and so we had Kyle Balda who's a, was a LucasArts alum, and at the time worked at ILM. He came over and gave us some 3-D pointers. At a couple of points we had some special private sessions.
coop0101	Dave: Oh, I wanted to also talk about-- I think this was, this may have been the first game, it may not have, but at LucasArts that I can remember, where you walk the character around and procedurally his head, Manny's head is turning towards--
coop0102	Paul: Oh, right.
coop0103	Dave: -- points of interest.
coop0104	Mark: Yeah.
coop0105	Dave: Procedurally!
coop0106	Mark: It was the first head tracking, right, at LucasArts?
coop0107	Dave: With a computer!
coop0108	Alex: Oh, definitely at LucasArts it was, for sure.
coop0109	Mark: I think that was because Tim didn't want to have like the UI highlighting stuff, right? Or he wanted to try something new.
coop0110	Dave: Yeah, it was partial hint and partial probably red herring, right?
coop0111	Mark: Oh.
coop0112	Dave: Like just show the player that they're in, point them in the direction of interesting subjects in the environment.
coop0113	Alex: And I mean, it shows you everything you can interact with, and not everything you can interact with is a solution, either.
coop0114	Peter T: There's no UI in this game, if anyone didn't notice, and that was by design.
coar0101	Peter M: I love the pulling the stocking down, and the leg bone being sexy. That's awesome.
