coar0108	Tim: "Go ahead and scratch." Um, I was thinking more of "Popeye." Olive Oyl falls into cement and she steps out of cement and everyone's whistling at her 'cause she has shapely legs and the cement falls away and she has Olive Oyl legs.
coar0109	Peter M: Oh I forgot that.
coar0110	Tim: That's what I always think of when I think of this scene.
coar0111	Peter M: There it is.
coar0201	Kevin: Yeah, so people, you know, a lot of people may know this already, but for those that don't: the game is just a big pre-rendered picture. There's no 3-D geometry in the game that describes the walls and the doors and the tables and all the props that are in the game. That's just a picture, a painting that's drawn in the background. So somebody had to go in manually and kind of make what we call walk boxes, but they're in 3-D space. They're like a little model that just describes, in 3-D space, where the, where Manny's allowed to walk. And if you turn the background off they just look like they're floating in space. There's nothing there. And there's boxes for walking, there's boxes for shadows, there's boxes for triggers that trigger, like, footstep sounds and things like that. But yeah, the image you see on the screen when you're playing the game, there is no real geometry there, so getting the illusion that Manny can walk in between tables and bounce off of walls and things like that is all smoke and mirrors.
coar0301	Peter M: See, I like that you still are not quite sure about Meche.
coar0302	Tim: That happens a lot in those noir movies.
coar0303	Peter M: Totally, it's just like--
coar0304	Tim: The woman is like, wait, is she in leagues with the bad guy, or is she being controlled and being blackmailed by the bad guy?
coar0305	Peter M: Yes. A testament to the fact that they're written by men.
coar0306	Tim: Ha ha ha ha ha... nervous laughter.
coil0101	Tim: Oh no, all the sprouted sailors. But what happens to their souls when they're sprouted?
coil0102	Peter M: I was wondering that myself.
coil0103	Tim: I think the idea was that they take the flowers and bring them back to the Land of the Living. They, like, get recycled.
coil0104	Peter M: Ah, they--
coil0105	Tim: I never really thought it through.
coil0106	Peter M: -- they get reincarnated which is sort of what you don't wanna do, if you're [dead].
comn0101	Kevin: One of the things with Manny's head and the look-at stuff was, um, it really meant that you couldn't put a lot of inventory items very close to each other, or limited the number of things in a room because even though you could toggle through and he would look at things kind of in sequence, it could get tricky if you put like three things on a table. Is he looking at thing number one, thing number two, or thing number three? So that was an interesting thing 'cause Lucas[Arts] was known for these adventure games with very dense-- Yeah, lots of things to click on in the screen. And you really couldn't do that here, but the game doesn't really feel lacking for it.
comn0102	Bret: I feel like that's filled in with dialogue. I mean ultimately you'd have those hunt-the-pixel games, you'd have a million things and you'd have to have one line for each thing. And with this one it's more like there are fewer things but there's more to say about each one, or more context or Manny kind of thinking to himself about things. It's a little more ruminating. So I think it adds to the character, just in terms of how much you kind of understand Manny or empathize with him.
comn0201	Lleslle: And do you remember the "Jesus bug?"
comn0202	Clint: No.
comn0203	Lleslle: The one where, when Manny would come on the water and he would float around, all over the screen? And so it would just randomly show up. And that was this bug that could not be squashed, that we think we'd kill and it'd come back. And talking about just trying to get it done, right? We were around the clock, it was towards the end. And literally we were, some of us were sleeping there and Bret Mogilefsky was working around the clock. And finally it was Tim, Steve Dauterman, myself, and then Bret's like, "I gotta go home now. I'm gonna go home now and they're going to test the new build." You know, and I was like, "Okay, go home. Get some sleep." It was like two or three in the morning. And then he just got home, and the "Jesus bug" came back.
comn0204	Clint: Ohh.
comn0205	Lleslle: Yeah! And then we were sitting in Steve Dauterman's office. And then I was on the couch and we were like, "Well, who's gonna call Bret?" And Steve looks at me and he's like, "You're the production manager." And it was like, oh gah!
comn0206	Clint: "Hey Bret, how's it going?"
comn0207	Lleslle: I know. "Hey, hi, can you come back? Thanks!"
comn0301	Tim: Reciprocating chisel... This scene is kind of actually right out of "The Fountainhead," this really-- Um, I think it's, is it Gary Cooper, or um... someone like that. And he's doing this super manly, like, drilling into this wall and the love interest of it comes over and is watching him, getting all aroused in a kind of a nineteen-thirties kind of way. And she's so mad that she's like-- She's out riding her horse and she slaps her riding crop onto her leg, show[s] her frustration. It's this hilarious scene. But for some reason I really like this jackhammer that he's just going into this wall with. And so it's here in a very similar erotic content here between Manny and Chepito.
comn0302	Peter M: Yeah, it's like I can see the... I can see the brotherly love.
cops0101	Dave: I wanted to point out that this octopus that we're seeing underwater was, I think, one of the things that Eric Ingerson took on.
cops0102	Mark: I was gonna say that, yeah.
cops0103	Dave: And it is-- That, back in 1997, that's probably like an animator's worst nightmare right there, I think. Trying to animate all those tentacles and not having a really intricate rig to work and keep all the curves in the arms. I'm not quite sure if Eric animated the tentacles frame-by-frame and bone-by-bone. I think he may have actually spent more time working on a rig to get what he wanted than he did animating.
cops0104	Mark: Yeah, he definitely set that character up, and he worked on that animal. And I think he did the spiders too, weren't we saying?
cops0105	Dave: I think Chris Schultz did some of that.
cops0106	Mark: Oh, Chris Schultz did the spiders.
cops0107	Dave: He may have rigged it, maybe.
cops0108	Mark: But Eric, he was really good at rigging and setting up the really complicated characters so that they would animate well.
cops0109	Dave: I think I remember Chris Schultz having some kind of elation going on when he got the spider rig 'cause he thought it was cool the way it worked.
cops0201	Kevin: You know, Tim's really into black velvet.
cops0202	Bret: Yeah.
cops0203	Kevin: And when I showed up my first day at work, there was a black velvet... kind of canvas, I don't know what it was. It was like this kind of like, you'd win it at the fair kind of thing. And it was like the grim reaper on a motorcycle with Mount Fuji in the background with these eagles flying out of it. And it was just pinned to the wall above my desk on the project. And for the entire project we really didn't talk much about it. It was just kind of there. And then when I moved on to the next project after it, I left my desk exactly the way I had found it, with the thing pinned up to the wall and the whole thing. And then Tim came and gave it to me. I still have it in my office right now. But he was like, "That's the mural that binds together 'Full Throttle' and 'Grim Fandango'--
cops0204	Bret: Interesting.
cops0205	Kevin: "--because it's the grim reaper on a motorcycle."
coea0101	Tim: And this is the part of the game I feel like it a lot more inspired by, actually, "City of Lost Children."
coea0102	Peter M: --"Children," yeah, right.
coea0103	Tim: That came out around that time, had a big influence on us. I think that's why we have a marching underwater diver, too. Like there's a lot of scenes in "City of Lost Children" where there's a diver just marching on the floor of the ocean, and it just seemed so dramatic and just he made this slow and steady pace, like... Slow and steady progress, but he was so heavy, and... and thick.
coea0104	Peter M: No flying fleas, though.
coea0105	Tim: No flying fleas.
cobu0101	Dave: I can't even imagine what was going through [Tim's] head at this point 'cause just the size of this game... Being able to absorb it all before it's realized.
cobu0102	Mark: He always had a really good idea of where we were and what was going on. And we were talking earlier about how there really wasn't a schedule that I remember, an art schedule for this game. It almost seemed like we just all sort of chose tasks and then we had Lleslle [Schroeder] sort of following up to make sure that we were... Doing them in about as much time as they should take, or not getting bogged down or stuck.
#year4commentary	
cosi0401	Dave: I have to say, coming out of school I felt super lucky to first of all be hired by LucasArts, by some colleagues of mine who went to the same school. And to walk into this building and see all the concept art for this game on the walls, I was like a kid in a candy store. It was instant euphoric inspiration. And um, I just felt like the luckiest guy in the world coming out of school, getting to work on a project of this caliber. It was beautiful.
colx0401	Tim: Yeah, the sprouting came later. It was like a solution to a problem we were having narratively with the game, which is like how do you make anything matter in the Land of the Dead? Like if you're going up against mobsters in a movie, usually you're afraid they're going to kill you or someone you love, right? Or break your thumbs. And in the Land of the Dead, who cares, you're dead, right? So we're like, "How do you kill people in the Land of the Dead?" And so we wanted to have it be that you were essentially brought back to life, like it went the other direction. You were returned to flowers. Um, and then flowers become this symbol of death in a weird way in the Land of the Dead. But flowers are such a huge part of the Day of the Dead, like the marigolds that you use in the Day of the Dead celebration, and the smell of marigolds are like a big part of the ceremony, so it was nice to have them be part of the game, too. And then the end of the game we wanted to have this thing called "the meadow," which was just full of flowers, which hopefully by that time would just look like death to you.
colm0201	Peter M: Tim, how come you know so much about neurosis and psychosis? Really...
colm0202	Tim: I don't know if--
colm0203	Peter M: It's quite striking.
colm0204	Tim: I don't know if any of this is legitimate psychological knowledge, or just what I learned from movies.
colm0205	Peter M: Well, speaking as sort of a crazy guy myself, it rings true to me.
cosd0301	Bret: Actually, I think it was funny when you came in and you were all excited about working on this game like, "I really like adventure games! I wanna do this kind of thing, I worked on all my own games." And I think about TellTale and it's like, okay, yeah that's pretty much in keeping. This was a long time ago, but I remember when you came in your game that you had worked on was like a helicopter game...?
cosd0302	Kevin: "Helicops!" Yes.
cosd0303	Bret: And it was like, as far different from this as you could possibly imagine a game would be, and I would not have imagined that a person who worked on "Helicops" was actually interested in adventure games.
cosd0304	Kevin: There weren't any games like this. All I wanted to do was play with little people in computers and I had gotten to the point where I almost ended up working on a baseball game 'cause that was the closest thing I could find to little people in computers. Um, and that's when I saw this. I was just like, it doesn't matter. I have to work on this.
coxb0101	Tim: Someone told me, like, this was the moment [when] they cried in the game. Just hearing how scared Glottis was in this scene, 'cause he gets really frightened. And it's actually a reference to a claim that a babysitter made about me when they were babysitting me. They said, "Oh, gee, you were so scared when your parents-- You would sit there and you'd say, 'Mother, mother, I'm frightened!'" And I was like, "No, okay first of all I never said that. I never said 'mother.'"
coxb0102	Peter M: Yeah, that's suspicious right there.
coxb0103	Tim: But it got to be this family joke. "Mother, I'm frightened! I'm frightened, mother!" And so that line actually got in the game.
cohb0101	Bret: Well I think it's one of the real challenges for adventure games because so many things you make for an adventure game are single use. Like single use animation, single use scenes, single use textures. They're all like, you do a first-person shooter, or you do, you know, adventure ga-- Oh, sorry, like a platformer or something like that. There are so many animations that get re-used so many times throughout the whole game, in an adventure game a huge amount of it is custom throwaway meant to be seen once or twice and occasionally all the time like walk cycles. But, you know it's not like "Mario." You know at the time they were doing "Mario 64" and it's like Mario has, you know, a ridiculous number of animations but it's a finite set and it's like they can all be loaded at all times. In this game, you have to dynamically load all the animations as they're needed, you have to make sure they're available before their triggers so you don't get these glitches while you wait for them. And a lot of them are just, they're thrown away. Every single one has to be individually processed by a human to get them into the game and potentially rigged up. And that's one of the challenges of making adventure games, is that you have all these sort of custom things and you want to reuse them as much as possible but in order to do that you have to automate a lot of stuff. Automate as much processing as you can. But you still have to have that ability to fix things. You know, in film, "We'll fix it in post." It's the same kind of thing. It's like, "We'll fix it in the engine. We'll fix it in the tool." As opposed to, "We'll fix it in the source art."
cohb0102	Kevin: And that's the uh, you know, like, you could walk to any room you want, and like all the little one-off assets? I think that's a unique challenge, technically, to adventure games that a lot of other games... Other genres don't really have to address. We're constantly loading in custom stuff and kicking custom stuff out, and on this game memory is tight. You walk into this room and we need sixteen-bit flipbooks of doors opening and closing, and one-off animations of "you may go and play the piano," "you may not go and play the piano." And there's all this technical kind of tomfoolery going on of trying to get the content that may be, that somebody-- Might be used, might not be used. You might just be running through this room to some other room. And it's not like loading up a level in a first-person shooter where you're like, "Okay, I-- I know what's going to be in front of the user at any one point in time. And we're going to use the same bad guy fifty times, over-- So I just need to get the stuff to run the bad guy in memory." This was a constant dance of like, just in time loading and management of stuff... because, uh... You could walk into this room and spend ten minutes in this room and explore every bit of content that this room could offer you, or you could just be running through on your way to somewhere else.
cohb0103	Bret: Back to this other puzzle.
cohb0104	Kevin: Yep.
cohb0105	Bret: Yeah.
