conq0103	Dave: Thank you, man!
conq0104	Alex: "Closed."
conq0105	Peter T: I think we ran into the polygon limit and we could only have so many. The pigeons--
conq0106	Dave: Were alpha flats. There were many, many alpha flats.
conq0107	Peter T: Yeah, but there were a lot of them, and they were actually some of the highest poly things in the game, so...
conq0108	Dave: It's my fault.
conq0109	Peter T: 'cause I think every bone got built on the pigeons.
conq0110	Dave: They were actually really fun to animate.
conq0111	Peter T: Yeah, the pigeons were a real fiasco. Sorry, Alex.
conq0112	Dave: It's kind of hard-- It was my first experience--
conq0113	Alex: I appreciate-- This finally brings come closure to the pigeon situation.
conq0114	Peter T: I'm answering your bug right now.
conq0115	Mark: Let's close this bug out.
conq0116	Dave: That was also my first experience animating something and not knowing how it was going to be used, so that was kind of like a different mental shift for me... Um... And it was just the first time I had had to make a bunch of idles, and loops or, you know, take-offs and lands that all had to work together. It was pretty interesting. I remember swearing.
conq0201	Darragh: I remember one day Tony [Plana] came into the studio and he had a silk shirt on and on top of the silk shirt he had a sort of a little sweater... a sleeveless sweater. And we were recording, it was like, "Gah, what are we hearing? What are we hearing?" And the engineer's like, "Oh, I think it's his sweater." We're like, "Hey, Tony, can you take your sweater off?" Takes his sweater off, back to rolling again. Sound's still there. So the-- He took his sweater off, and went back to rolling. It turned out it was his shirt that was making the noise, and we're like, "Tony, it's the shirt! It's the silk shirt." And so he's like, "It's okay, I got a T-shirt on underneath." So he was down--
conq0202	Julian: Just stripping down!
conq0203	Darragh: -- down, yeah, he was stripping down into his T-shirt, and did the rest of the session in his T-shirt. But that was the thing with Tony, is he just loved-- You know, he loved working. For part of the production he was shooting at night on a movie, and there were a couple of days where he came in and he only had like a couple of hours, you know a few hours of sleep. And I remember saying, like, "I can reschedule the session." And he's like, "No no no, let's do it, let's do it." And he always was there to give it-- Yeah, giving it his all.
conq0301	Tim: One of the standard things that happens in noir is that a guy meets a woman that just spirals him into a world of shadows and intrigue and stuff like that. And sometimes it's because it's a femme fatale who's up to no good, and sometimes it's also a totally innocent woman who just happens to be on this path that she's-- She's in sort of trouble and he gets pulled into it. So you meet Meche in this world and it spins Manny off into this whole different path, but it's really-- For some reason it was important to me that Manny was actually partially to blame, or feel like he was partially to blame. Like he thought he had caused her to be lost, when it was actually Domino's fault that she was sent off. He stole her Number Nine Ticket.
conq0302	Peter M: Well, you didn't want to blame the woman, like say "Double Indemnity" does, or--
conq0303	Tim: Yeah, where she was actually scheming and where-- Yeah, this is more someone who Manny feels like he has caused misfortune to come to.
conq0304	Peter M: Or "Maltese Falcon" does for that matter, yeah.
conq0401	Tim: I haven't done it in too many games, but having a year transition between things and changing the location and letting you pick up friends and travel, like, just allows you to have so much character development where Manny and Glottis have now been through a lot of stuff together and they've done... They're in different clothes, which is-- They're in different jobs and they move from town to town together and just, it starts to build this real friendship between them, which is... nice, and I think I realize I'm going to put four year transitions in all games from now on, just so we can have that kind of relationship building.
conq0501	Lleslle: Yeah, I remember having to get extensions. That was like my favorite--
conq0502	Julian: Oh yeah.
conq0503	Lleslle: No, I would have to go in 'cause I'd be the same thing, because I always advocate for the artist, and I did. I mean, that's why I loved working with Tim. He's so creative. And so we would talk and collaborate, and then when I'm like, "No, look, this is the date," 'cause I'm trying to save the date, and he did the ship story. And so then we had to go get more time, and I was like, "Well, how are we going to do that?" And then he was like, "Here's my technique. I go into Jack's office and I stay in there and I threaten to light myself on fire, and I just scream and I stay in there until he extends the date." And I'm like, "That's a great... strategy."
conq0504	Julian: It worked.
conq0505	Lleslle: I know! Threaten to light myself on fire--
conq0506	Clint: Well, see we would never try that in the sound department because Jack would just say, "Go ahead."
conq0507	Julian: Yeah, yeah!
